  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">STRENGTHENING GRASSLAND CONSERVATION IN</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Mexico, only 5.6 percent of the grasslands are under protection. Janos and Mapimi are two Grassland Priority Conservation Areas (GPCAs) that have Biosphere Reserves, the highest protection category in the world. There, 4.8 percent of the grasslands of the Chihuahuan Desert and 85.5 percent of the grasslands in Natural Protected Areas (NPA) in Mexico are protected. These areas offer habitat for about 35 species of grassland birds, including Chestnut-collared Longspur, Sprague&apos;s Pipit and Thick-billed Longspur. The threats they face are the unproductiveness of their rangelands, the change in land use and lack of training for producers, all these factors cause the loss of habitat for these bird species. This proposal seeks to reinforce and support the process of harmonizing conservation with productivity that these two NPAs have been doing for more than 10 years, for the recovery of pastures, using a socio-ecological framework, taking into account the opinion of the producers and their needs. This project will increase the conservation agreements to 168,714 hectares of these two GPCAs and will impact 10,278 hectares of critical habitat for key species with restoration and management actions. Partners will also carry out an extension program for the training of 40 producers in better livestock practices. Bird monitoring will be carried out to know the baseline of the ranches and see the effect of conservation actions. Finally, a constant campaign will be carried out on social networks to continue with the dissemination of information on grasslands and their importance, in addition to management and participation with Federal Agencies, State and Municipal Governments, continental initiatives such as the Central Grassland Roadmap, with the purpose of supporting and proposing the recovery of grasslands on the local and national agenda.</narrative>
  </description>
